WEBVTT
NOTE how-is-3d-printed-metal-made - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.189
Laser powder bed fusion can melt metal
powder in layers as thin as 20 microns.

00:00:05.189 --> 00:00:07.469
That is thinner than a human hair.

00:00:07.469 --> 00:00:10.955
But the thinness is not what makes the
process work.

00:00:10.955 --> 00:00:15.223
What makes it work is that each layer
bonds metallurgically to the layer below

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:18.288
it — meaning the atoms interdiffuse across
the boundary.

00:00:18.288 --> 00:00:20.327
You are not gluing layers together.

00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:24.405
You are creating a continuous solid from
repeated, controlled melting.

00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:26.661
How is 3D printed metal made?

00:00:26.661 --> 00:00:31.318
Metal powder for additive manufacturing is
not the same as powder for pressing or

00:00:31.318 --> 00:00:31.893
sintering.

00:00:31.893 --> 00:00:37.197
It must be spherical, because spherical
particles flow predictably and pack

00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:37.904
uniformly.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:43.439
Most powder is made by gas atomization —
molten metal is broken into droplets by

00:00:43.439 --> 00:00:47.659
high-pressure inert gas, and the droplets
solidify in flight.

00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:49.461
The size distribution matters.

00:00:49.461 --> 00:00:54.448
If the range is too wide, fine particles
fill the voids between large ones, and the

00:00:54.448 --> 00:00:56.251
powder does not spread evenly.

00:00:56.251 --> 00:01:02.428
A typical specification for titanium alloy
powder is 15 to 45 microns, with less than

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:04.608
10 percent outside that range.

00:01:04.608 --> 00:01:08.868
The median particle size for
aerospace-grade titanium alloy powder is

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:13.993
around 25 microns, though the acceptable
range is 15 to 60 microns depending on the

00:01:13.993 --> 00:01:15.845
machine and the part geometry.

00:01:15.845 --> 00:01:21.512
Finer powder gives better surface finish
but is harder to handle safely — it can be

00:01:21.512 --> 00:01:22.741
pyrophoric in air.

00:01:22.741 --> 00:01:27.709
Coarser powder flows more reliably but
limits the minimum feature size you can

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:28.091
print.

00:01:28.091 --> 00:01:33.577
The powder must also have low oxygen
content, typically below 1300 parts per

00:01:33.577 --> 00:01:38.125
million for titanium, because oxygen
embrittles the final part.

00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:42.502
In laser powder bed fusion, a thin layer
of powder is spread across the build

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.776
platform by a recoating blade or roller.

00:01:44.776 --> 00:01:49.988
A laser beam, typically 50 to 100 watts
for small machines and up to 400 watts for

00:01:49.988 --> 00:01:54.819
production systems, scans across the
powder bed following a programmed path.

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:58.291
Where the beam hits, the powder melts and
forms a melt pool.

00:01:58.291 --> 00:02:04.173
The melt pool is typically 100 to 200
microns wide and lasts only milliseconds.

00:02:04.173 --> 00:02:07.235
When the beam moves on, the metal
solidifies.

00:02:07.235 --> 00:02:12.882
The platform drops by one layer thickness,
a new layer of powder is spread, and the

00:02:12.882 --> 00:02:13.971
process repeats.

00:02:13.971 --> 00:02:19.588
Laser systems operate in an inert gas
atmosphere — argon or nitrogen — to

00:02:19.588 --> 00:02:20.973
prevent oxidation.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:26.133
Electron beam systems operate in vacuum,
which eliminates oxidation entirely but

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:28.520
requires the powder to be conductive.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.479
Electron beam melting is faster because
the beam can be deflected

00:02:32.479 --> 00:02:37.473
electromagnetically with no moving parts,
but it is limited to metals that conduct

00:02:37.473 --> 00:02:39.117
electricity in powder form.

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:41.379
The scan pattern is not arbitrary.

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:46.502
Within each layer, the laser traces
parallel lines called hatch lines, spaced

00:02:46.502 --> 00:02:49.629
to ensure overlap between adjacent melt
tracks.

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:55.512
Between layers, the hatch direction is
rotated — commonly by 67 or 90 degrees —

00:02:55.512 --> 00:02:59.533
to avoid building up columnar grains in
one direction.

00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:03.933
A contour pass around the edge of each
layer improves surface finish.

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:08.382
The local thermal gradient and cooling
rate determine the microstructure.

00:03:08.382 --> 00:03:12.952
In titanium alloys, rapid cooling produces
a fine acicular alpha structure.

00:03:12.952 --> 00:03:18.000
In nickel superalloys, it can produce
columnar grains aligned with the build

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:18.664
direction.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:21.753
The thermal history is not uniform across
the part.

00:03:21.753 --> 00:03:26.295
Regions near the substrate cool faster
because heat conducts into the build

00:03:26.295 --> 00:03:26.840
platform.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.998
Regions in the center of a large
cross-section cool more slowly because

00:03:30.998 --> 00:03:33.107
they are surrounded by hot material.

00:03:33.107 --> 00:03:37.959
This means microstructure varies with
position, and post-process heat treatment

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:40.600
is almost always required to homogenize
it.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.001
Cooling rates in laser powder bed fusion
can reach 1000 to 10000 degrees Celsius

00:03:46.001 --> 00:03:49.917
per second, which is far faster than
conventional casting.

00:03:49.917 --> 00:03:55.149
I wanted to say the layers get welded
together, because that's the intuitive

00:03:55.149 --> 00:04:00.577
They don't weld, they fuse metallurgically
— the atoms interdiffuse across the

00:04:00.577 --> 00:04:03.987
boundary. Wrong verb, wrong physics, so I
cut it.

00:04:03.987 --> 00:04:08.124
Porosity is the most common defect in
metal additive manufacturing.

00:04:08.124 --> 00:04:13.064
It comes from insufficient energy density
— if the laser power is too low or the

00:04:13.064 --> 00:04:17.325
scan speed too high, the powder does not
fully melt and voids remain.

00:04:17.325 --> 00:04:21.786
It comes from gas trapped in the powder
particles during atomization.

00:04:21.786 --> 00:04:26.636
It comes from keyhole collapse — at very
high energy density, the melt pool

00:04:26.636 --> 00:04:31.549
vaporizes and creates a deep narrow cavity
that can collapse and trap vapor.

00:04:31.549 --> 00:04:36.801
Porosity above 0.5 percent by volume
typically disqualifies a part for

00:04:36.801 --> 00:04:39.352
structural aerospace applications.

00:04:39.352 --> 00:04:42.845
The acceptable porosity depends on the
application.

00:04:42.845 --> 00:04:46.749
Aerospace structural parts require less
than 0.2 percent.

00:04:46.749 --> 00:04:52.223
Medical implants can tolerate around 1.0
percent if the pores are small and

00:04:52.223 --> 00:04:53.464
well-distributed.

00:04:53.464 --> 00:04:57.731
Tooling and non-critical components can
tolerate 2.0 percent.

00:04:57.731 --> 00:05:03.396
Porosity is measured by X-ray computed
tomography or by metallographic sectioning

00:05:03.396 --> 00:05:04.725
and image analysis.

00:05:04.725 --> 00:05:09.964
The measurement method matters — CT can
detect pores as small as 10 microns, while

00:05:09.964 --> 00:05:13.797
sectioning only samples a small fraction
of the part volume.

00:05:13.797 --> 00:05:18.824
Any surface tilted more than about 45
degrees from vertical requires support

00:05:18.824 --> 00:05:19.552
structures.

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:24.859
This is because the powder below an
unsupported overhang cannot conduct heat

00:05:24.859 --> 00:05:28.560
away fast enough, and the melt pool sags
or distorts.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.163
Supports are printed from the same
material as the part, using the same

00:05:33.163 --> 00:05:37.248
process, and they must be mechanically
removed after the build.

00:05:37.248 --> 00:05:41.247
Removal is done by machining, wire EDM, or
manual grinding.

00:05:41.247 --> 00:05:46.127
Support removal adds cost and limits the
geometric freedom that additive

00:05:46.127 --> 00:05:48.635
manufacturing is supposed to provide.

00:05:48.635 --> 00:05:53.495
Part orientation during the build is a
design decision that trades support volume

00:05:53.495 --> 00:05:56.075
against surface finish and residual
stress.

00:05:56.075 --> 00:06:01.724
After printing, the part is removed from
the build platform — usually by wire EDM

00:06:01.724 --> 00:06:02.491
or bandsaw.

00:06:02.491 --> 00:06:07.367
It is then stress-relieved to reduce
residual stresses from the thermal cycling

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.416
during the build.

00:06:08.416 --> 00:06:13.936
Stress relief for titanium alloys is
typically 650 to 800 degrees Celsius for

00:06:13.936 --> 00:06:15.227
two to four hours.

00:06:15.227 --> 00:06:20.470
For critical applications, hot isostatic
pressing is used to close internal

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:21.099
porosity.

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:26.316
HIP applies high temperature and high
pressure — typically 900 degrees Celsius

00:06:26.316 --> 00:06:31.467
and 100 megapascals for titanium — which
collapses voids and improves fatigue

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:36.016
properties. Final machining brings
critical dimensions to tolerance.

00:06:36.016 --> 00:06:41.935
Hot isostatic pressing for titanium alloys
is typically conducted at 900 degrees

00:06:41.935 --> 00:06:47.115
Celsius under 100 megapascals of argon
pressure for two to four hours.

00:06:47.115 --> 00:06:51.564
This closes porosity below the detection
limit of most inspection methods and

00:06:51.564 --> 00:06:55.781
brings tensile and fatigue properties
close to those of wrought material.

00:06:55.781 --> 00:07:01.211
The improvement in fatigue life can be a
factor of two or more, which is why HIP is

00:07:01.211 --> 00:07:03.435
standard for aerospace components.

00:07:03.435 --> 00:07:08.981
Wire arc additive manufacturing uses a
welding arc to melt metal wire, which is

00:07:08.981 --> 00:07:10.736
deposited layer by layer.

00:07:10.736 --> 00:07:16.415
It is much faster than powder bed methods
— deposition rates can exceed 10 kilograms

00:07:16.415 --> 00:07:21.419
per hour compared to 50 to 100 grams per
hour for laser powder bed fusion.

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:25.797
It is used for large structural parts in
aerospace and shipbuilding.

00:07:25.797 --> 00:07:27.536
The trade-off is precision.

00:07:27.536 --> 00:07:32.405
Layer thickness is typically 1 to 3
millimeters, and surface finish is rough.

00:07:32.405 --> 00:07:36.006
Wire arc parts almost always require
extensive machining.

00:07:36.006 --> 00:07:40.869
The process is essentially robotic welding
applied to additive manufacturing.

00:07:40.869 --> 00:07:45.906
The numbers in this script — layer
thickness, particle size, laser power,

00:07:45.906 --> 00:07:51.150
porosity limits, HIP conditions — come
from ASTM standards, NIST measurement

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.048
programs, and ASM International handbooks.

00:07:54.048 --> 00:07:58.388
These are the bodies that publish
consensus data for industrial metal

00:07:58.388 --> 00:07:59.835
additive manufacturing.

00:07:59.835 --> 00:08:04.950
When a range is given, it reflects real
variation across alloys, machines, and

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:05.803
applications.

00:08:05.803 --> 00:08:11.632
When a single figure is stated, it is a
typical midpoint, not a universal

00:08:11.632 --> 00:08:17.306
The cooling rate range of 1000 to 10000
degrees Celsius per second is measured by

00:08:17.306 --> 00:08:19.968
high-speed pyrometry during the build.

00:08:19.968 --> 00:08:24.214
This script describes the process and the
parameters, but it does not tell you

00:08:24.214 --> 00:08:28.677
whether a given part design is suitable
for additive manufacturing, or whether the

00:08:28.677 --> 00:08:30.800
properties will meet your requirements.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.506
That depends on the alloy, the geometry,
the thermal history, the post-processing,

00:08:35.506 --> 00:08:37.056
and the inspection methods.

00:08:37.056 --> 00:08:42.763
The process is controllable, but it is not
yet as repeatable as casting or forging,

00:08:42.763 --> 00:08:46.064
and every production program involves
iteration.

00:08:46.064 --> 00:08:50.561
The published porosity limits are for
qualification, not for every part that

00:08:50.561 --> 00:08:51.744
comes off a machine.

00:08:51.744 --> 00:09:01.346
Residual stress is stress that remains in
a part after the external forces are

00:09:01.346 --> 00:09:02.331
removed.

00:09:02.331 --> 00:09:07.918
In metal additive manufacturing, it builds
up because each new layer heats the layers

00:09:07.918 --> 00:09:11.861
below it, causing expansion, and then
contracts as it cools.

00:09:11.861 --> 00:09:17.090
The layers below resist this contraction,
and tensile stress accumulates.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:22.820
Residual stresses in as-built laser powder
bed fusion parts can reach 200 to 400

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:28.549
megapascals in titanium alloys and 400 to
800 megapascals in nickel superalloys.

00:09:28.549 --> 00:09:32.671
These values approach the yield strength
of the material, which is why parts

00:09:32.671 --> 00:09:36.629
sometimes crack during the build or warp
when removed from the substrate.

00:09:36.629 --> 00:09:42.088
The magnitude depends on the alloy, the
part geometry, and the scan strategy.

00:09:42.088 --> 00:09:47.433
A typical measured value is 300
megapascals for titanium alloy and 600

00:09:47.433 --> 00:09:50.029
megapascals for nickel superalloy.

00:09:50.029 --> 00:09:54.247
Long, thin walls accumulate more stress
than compact geometries.

00:09:54.247 --> 00:09:59.322
Scanning the same region repeatedly
without allowing it to cool increases the

00:09:59.322 --> 00:10:00.179
local stress.

00:10:00.179 --> 00:10:05.076
Some machines preheat the build platform
to 200 degrees Celsius or higher to reduce

00:10:05.076 --> 00:10:08.675
the thermal gradient between the melt pool
and the substrate.

00:10:08.675 --> 00:10:12.318
Preheating reduces residual stress but
does not eliminate it.

00:10:12.318 --> 00:10:16.917
Stress relief heat treatment after the
build is mandatory for most structural

00:10:16.917 --> 00:10:17.693
applications.

00:10:17.693 --> 00:10:20.151
Powder is expensive, so it is reused.

00:10:20.151 --> 00:10:25.200
After a build, the unused powder is
recovered from the build chamber, sieved

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.382
to remove spatter — small solidified
droplets ejected from the melt pool — and

00:10:30.382 --> 00:10:31.245
agglomerates.

00:10:31.245 --> 00:10:35.489
The recovered powder is blended with
virgin powder for the next build, with

00:10:35.489 --> 00:10:36.904
monitoring at each cycle.

00:10:36.904 --> 00:10:39.194
But powder degrades with each cycle.

00:10:39.194 --> 00:10:43.964
Oxygen and nitrogen content increase from
exposure to the atmosphere during

00:10:43.964 --> 00:10:44.536
handling.

00:10:44.536 --> 00:10:49.434
Particle size distribution shifts as fine
particles are preferentially consumed and

00:10:49.434 --> 00:10:51.795
spatter adds coarse irregular particles.

00:10:51.795 --> 00:10:56.682
ASTM standards require tracking the number
of reuse cycles and testing powder

00:10:56.682 --> 00:11:00.109
chemistry and particle size distribution
periodically.

00:11:00.109 --> 00:11:03.154
The number of times powder can be reused
varies.

00:11:03.154 --> 00:11:08.545
For titanium alloys, 12 cycles is a common
industry limit, though some programs allow

00:11:08.545 --> 00:11:12.477
up to 20 if the powder passes chemistry
and flowability tests.

00:11:12.477 --> 00:11:18.689
For aluminum alloys, the limit is lower —
around 5 to 8 cycles — because aluminum

00:11:18.689 --> 00:11:20.376
oxidizes more readily.

00:11:20.376 --> 00:11:25.424
Powder that fails specification is either
discarded or sent back to the supplier for

00:11:25.424 --> 00:11:26.205
reprocessing.

00:11:26.205 --> 00:11:31.645
The cost of powder and the cost of testing
it are both significant fractions of the

00:11:31.645 --> 00:11:36.822
total part cost, which is why powder
management is a process control issue, not

00:11:36.822 --> 00:11:38.264
just a material issue.

00:11:38.264 --> 00:11:40.923
Metal additive manufacturing is not magic.

00:11:40.923 --> 00:11:44.911
It is controlled melting, repeated
thousands of times, with the

00:11:44.911 --> 00:11:48.520
microstructure and defects determined by
thermal history.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:52.113
The precision comes from the beam control
and the powder quality.

00:11:52.113 --> 00:11:56.369
The limitation comes from the fact that
you are melting and solidifying metal

00:11:56.369 --> 00:11:58.083
faster than equilibrium allows.
